WEBVTT

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:03.880
Support from viewers like you makes this program possible.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:05.840
Please give to your PBS station.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.410
Do you know what an elfous is?

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.160
It's a souffle spelt backwards.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.360
Well, backwards or forwards,

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.280
you won't want to miss this chocolate souffle

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.400
that we're doing next time on The French Chef.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.100
French Chef is made possible by a grant from the Polaroid Corporation.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.450
This is a maximum security oven.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.970
It's not to be open for 25 minutes.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.220
or everybody will be court-martialed.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.350
There's a souffle in it.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:08.390
We're doing chocolate souffle today on The French Chef.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:59.980
I'm a chef. I'm Julia Child. I don't dare open my maximum security oven because I've got a chocolate souffle in it. And in about 25 minutes, it should come out all puffed with pride. And so should I, I hope. I think I've never run into anyone who just doesn't adore chocolate souffle. But an awful lot of people are just scared to death to make it. Well, I don't think anyone should be scared to make anything because there isn't any trick at the

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.040
to making a souffle, as you're going to see.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.360
And besides, a chocolate souffle is such a nice dessert

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.980
to have up your sleeve.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.180
That's one of the best tricks of all.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:13.810
So, if you're going to make a souffle,

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:16.870
the first thing to do is to read the recipe

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.520
and then preheat your oven.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.900
Your oven, in this case, is to be preheated to 375 degrees

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:26.000
with the rack in the middle.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.500
And then, get out all the ingredients you want

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:29.960
in your pocket.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.720
and pans. You can go right to it. And the first item on our agenda is melting

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:43.740
chocolate. And for that we want to have it melted in some coffee because that

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:49.020
gives a nice mocha flavor. We want three tablespoons of strong coffee. So I'm

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:56.580
going to use three tablespoons of instant coffee and melt it in three

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.980
tablespoons of boiling

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:09.830
water. One, two, three. And then set it overheat a little bit so it's absolutely thoroughly

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:18.350
dissolved. And then we're ready to melt our chocolate. And you have to be very careful

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:23.810
when you're melting chocolate because if it's overheated, it will turn sort of dark and

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:29.780
granular. And perfectly melted chocolate should be a lovely, smooth velvet.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:36.320
And so I think the best system to do is to have your whatever you're melting your chocolate in

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:44.120
and set that in a pan of very hot water and then take the water off heat and then put the chocolate in.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:51.790
I'm using, I should have put this so fast in, I'm using these semi-sweet chocolate bits,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:53.930
which I like very much because they melt so easily.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:59.810
We want six ounces, that would be six ounces or squares of semi-sweet chocolate.

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:06.640
or one cup of the semi-sweet chocolate bits and then they're in the pan and the water is hot and

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:15.720
then just stir them around for a moment and then let the pan sit off heat and when you come back

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:22.960
to use your chocolate it'll be already melted you see that's already started in and if by any chance

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:29.240
you've overheated your chocolate and it becomes stiff and black you can sometimes bring it back by

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:29.980
a beating in a tank.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.360
a tablespoon of warm water.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.880
But if it's really gotten hard and granular,

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.880
you really have to throw it out

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.120
because it won't usually soften again.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.860
So just remember to melt your chocolate over very low heat

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.300
and then you won't have any trouble.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.460
And so now, after you have your chocolate melted,

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.800
you're going, you want to prepare the dish

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.560
that your souffles to bake in.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:04:59.970
And in France, a souffle dish is called

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.760
a mold, so we will be French and call it a mold. And this is a French souffle dish or

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:12.920
mold, which is called a charlotte, which is made out of fairly heavy tinned metal. And

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:20.360
this one is six inches in the diameter in the bottom and seven inches at the top, and

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.020
it's about four inches high, which gives a very nice drama. And this charlotte mold is

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.540
a very nice thing to have because you can do all kinds of things in it besides

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.080
But you have to get them either in France or at an import shop.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.840
So I'm not going to use my favorite charlotte.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:45.400
I'm going to use an American ovenproof, flame-proof casserole, which is very nice.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:51.030
This is a one and three quarter quart or seven cup casserole.

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:55.330
And it's about six and a half to seven inches in diameter here.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.550
And it's about three and a half inches high.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:58.910
So we'll use this.

00:05:58.910 --> 00:05:59.730
This is very good.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:08.280
baking dish and we want to you have to butter it this is the french method of doing things in some

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:13.750
uh other recipes you'll find that they say don't butter it but we're doing everything

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:19.780
in exactly the french way because i know it works and then that's thoroughly buttered on the inside

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:29.670
and then you want a little bit of cake flour or cornstarch and then that is to be turned all around

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:36.440
and then we knock out the excess but you want to be sure that you have cornstarch or whatever

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:42.940
your or cake flour lining all of it and then i'm going to dump that out and then bang it

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:53.340
so we don't have any excess and then the recipe we're going to do is um is one that'll serve six

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:59.920
to eight people and it should really cook in a two quart mold but this is one in three

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:07.500
quarters quarts and so we can make it larger by putting a collar onto it and for this take

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:29.920
some aluminum foil, you want a great big piece, circle the mold and then

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:39.010
double it. In other words make a long strip out of it. That's about six inches

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:44.020
high and then this again must be buttered because this is going to go

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:49.420
around the mold then the souffle will come up inside the collar and you have to

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:56.900
butter it so that when the souffle is done you can take the collar off. There

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.500
that's all buttered. Now we

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:04.860
set it around the mold you notice this mold has little ears on it so we have to

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:12.480
make some ear holes so just make a little depression in the aluminum foil and then

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:22.190
cut the ear holes this is a good system because you can really use almost any

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:29.260
size of dish for it except you don't want too wide a dish because if you do

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:29.980
you'll find

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.910
that the souffle won't rise very well, because there's too much of a mass.

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:45.760
Now we have the ear holes, and we bring this around, and then pin it in place.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.240
You can tie it, but I think pinning is easier.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:54.280
This is just a plain, ordinary, common pin, and be sure to pin it a bit below, and leave,

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.660
have the head on the bottom, because it'll be much easier to take out.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.960
If you have the head on the top, you may just...

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:08.560
the souffle. And so now our mold is all ready. This is just about six inches high. So remember

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:14.360
that for this recipe that we're doing, you want something that is six and a half, about

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:23.340
six inches high, and has about six and a half to seven inch diameter across. Now if you want

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:29.910
do this will souffle will serve say six to eight people if you want to serve

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.800
10 people, you're much better off cooking,

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.320
making double the recipe and cooking it in two molds.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.540
Because if you have a very large mold like that,

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.580
when you have this great mass of souffle

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:46.240
that has to come up, it's just too much of an effort for it.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.200
And often it just won't come up high.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.820
And we want as much drama as possible.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.000
And so now we have our mold ready.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.280
We can just set it aside and we're ready to do our souffle.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:03.790
And I think people are often just frightened at the name of souffle.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:08.610
They tremble, they feel it's difficult, mysterious, and it means collapse and disaster.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:12.830
But all a souffle is, and we've done several of them before,

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:20.870
it's a thick sauce base with a flavor in it, into which you fold beaten egg whites.

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:22.690
And that's all you have to do.

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:26.710
The egg whites do the rest, because once the souffle gets in the oven,

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.890
and the heat of the oven makes...

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.160
the egg whites automatically puff the souffle.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.480
So just remember, if you're worried about a souffle,

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.940
that that's all it is, and it is automatic.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:41.840
It's just the correct assembly, which I'm going to show you.

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:46.870
So we will now get to the rest of our souffle mixture,

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:50.570
which includes the souffle base.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.440
And the base of the souffle is a thick, white sauce.

00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.790
And we're going to use...

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:02.130
For the thickening, this is a milk sauce,

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:05.350
and for the thickening, we're going to use cornstarch.

00:11:05.510 --> 00:11:11.100
And we want one-third cup of sifted cornstarch.

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.950
And the easiest way to do is to sift it on waxed paper.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:20.850
And the reason that you sift it is because you otherwise

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.450
can't measure it properly.

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:27.460
So just put the cornstarch in the measure and sift,

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:29.860
sift right into your one-

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:37.950
third cup and then sweep off the excess with the straight edge of a knife and put it into

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:45.630
a saucepan and do do be sure and measure it that way because it's surprising what a difference

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:52.010
it makes whether or not the corn starch is sifted or not you can use either corn starch or potato

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:59.380
starch or rice starch it makes no difference but for this souffle we don't use flour and now with the

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:05.280
cornstarch we're going to have one and a half cups of milk and beat it in beat in

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.630
just a little bit at first because we want to be sure that the cornstarch

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:17.070
doesn't have any lumps in it or you could beat this all in an electric

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:23.050
blender if you want it but I don't think it's really necessary just be sure that

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:27.030
when you're beating it in you get your whip all around the edges of the pan

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:29.450
and give it a good beating and then

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:36.500
beat in the rest of the milk at this point you can set it over heat and beat in the rest of the milk

00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:48.790
and then we want one half cup of granulated sugar so in goes the sugar and then just stir it over

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:54.430
heat until the sauce thickens i'll go over those proportions again in case you didn't get them

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:59.980
That is one-third cup of sifted cornstarch and gradually...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.840
beat in one and one half cups of milk

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:06.180
and one half cup of granulated sugar

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:09.520
and beat over heat, or you don't have to beat,

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:11.600
it's just sort of a slow stirring over heat

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.620
until the sauce is thickened.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:16.890
Now this, I can't remember whether we've made

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:19.730
one of these cornstarch sauces before or not,

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:22.110
but when you're making a chocolate souffle,

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:26.210
you have a problem because the chocolate is heavy.

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.930
And if you use a regular sort of a white sauce base,

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.440
with flour and a butter roux, you will find that you end up with sort of a chocolate pudding

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:42.540
rather than a lovely light chocolate souffle. And so that is why in a chocolate souffle,

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:51.800
it's best to use cornstarch or rice starch or potato starch, because it makes all the

00:13:51.800 --> 00:13:57.050
difference in the delicacy. You see, she's beginning to steam up, and in just a moment,

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:57.790
when it's gonna thicken.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:04.980
I've tried doing this souffle base ahead and it doesn't seem to me that it works very well

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:12.360
because once the cornstarch is cooled off it gets to be rather like glue and then to reheat it again

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:18.240
it's sometimes difficult to get the lumps out so I actually think for this it's better

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:24.430
not to try and do the sauce base ahead because you have so much trouble getting all the getting the

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:29.890
cornstarch sort of the cold slab of cornstarch beaten and smooth

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:37.670
up again over a heat that you might as well have started from the beginning. Now you see that's

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:42.970
thickening up there and just stir it and be careful that you have a nice heavy pan so that

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:50.130
you won't scorch anything on the bottom. You see that's a thick glue and it hardly looks appetizing.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:59.460
But now that is all done and then you you're going to leave it for a little while and you don't want

00:14:59.460 --> 00:14:59.900
that

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:08.400
top of it to sort of film over or crust over. So we're going to have some butter in it.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:16.640
And so put the butter in. Right now we want three tablespoons of butter and that just

00:15:16.640 --> 00:15:24.350
goes right on the top. And let it spread out. The butter is very important if you don't

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:28.850
put the butter in. You have sort of a, there's just something lacking in the souffle.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:38.250
that you know that just sort of some special lovely taste which you have to have and then we'll

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:45.080
take a look at our chocolate and see how that's doing i'll take it out and stir it around see

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:53.100
that's coming up very nicely and it's really very easy to melt it this way because it's really all

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:59.190
melted by the time you come back to it so that the chocolate is melted

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.140
And our souffle base is done.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:07.450
So we're now ready to go on to the rest of the souffle.

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:12.520
So this all now has to go into a bowl

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:17.400
because this saucepan really isn't big enough

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:18.820
to do the rest of the mixing.

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:22.280
So in goes your sauce and butter,

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:26.340
and then in goes your melted chocolate.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:41.220
and that's all going to get stirred up when we put the eggs in so there's our chocolate and sauce

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:49.260
and we're going to now have some eggs yolks in our sauce and we want five egg yolks and then we're

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:55.800
going to have egg whites so i'll separate them together i really think it's a safe idea because

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:59.720
when you're going to beat egg whites which we have to have for this

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:04.640
You want to be sure that there isn't any egg yolk in the white.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:12.780
So I very often, unless I'm being very daring indeed, break the whites into a separate bowl.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:20.700
There, I can start beating these in a little bit.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:29.970
You don't want your chocolate to be too hot because...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.840
but then it would scramble your egg yolks.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:41.130
There, now we have, that was, how many did I have?

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.040
One, two, three, that was silly, I'm talking too much.

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.280
This is our third egg.

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:55.060
Trouble very often if you're beating,

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:57.040
if you're breaking eggs,

00:17:57.080 --> 00:17:59.980
you can put the wrong thing into the wrong dish.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:09.790
That's four, and here is our fifth and final egg.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:24.320
There. Now, the egg yolks get beaten up into this

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:29.980
so it's nicely mixed, and then we're ready.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.010
to beat the egg whites. And we want to have seven egg whites. We had five yolks in the

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:42.930
chocolate mixer. We want seven egg whites. We have five in here now. And you can freeze

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:48.690
egg whites perfectly well and thaw them out. And two egg whites would be one-fourth cup.

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:55.670
So just measure out one-fourth cup. And now we have seven egg whites. I'm going to beat

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:59.710
these by hand just because I like to. You could perfectly well beat them in a machine.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:06.040
I have an unlined copper bowl and a large balloon whip.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:09.040
See, I have still a little egg white there.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:16.070
And so start in, this would be the same as to whether you're beating them by hand or machine.

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:23.520
And we have two extra egg whites in here just to make the souffle lighter and rise higher.

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:28.980
So whether you're beating by hand or machine, you start out rather slowly at first.

00:19:30.220 --> 00:19:36.830
until the eggs are broken up and then if you're beating by machine you would put in for this

00:19:36.830 --> 00:19:46.290
amount a full quarter teaspoon of cream of tartar and that keeps the eggs uh standing up and stiffly

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:51.790
beaten after you've beaten them with the copper bowl you don't need to have any cream of tartar

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:58.120
but you always want a little bit of salt that's about a eighth of a teaspoon of salt then start

00:19:58.120 --> 00:19:58.580
Beating.

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:14.730
You can beat up and down and around and around.

00:20:17.420 --> 00:20:21.210
It really goes very quickly and it's also good exercise.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:23.270
You notice you're not beating from your shoulder,

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:24.690
you're beating from your elbow.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.350
The idea is to beat as much, just as much air into the egg bites as possible.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:45.490
And now when they form soft peaks, to make them even a little stiffer, you beat in a bit of sugar.

00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:52.540
Beat in about a tablespoon and a half of sugar and that just stiffens them up a little bit more.

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:04.300
Now I have, they aren't quite beaten up enough yet.

00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:07.170
Maybe a little bit with my other hand.

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:14.200
These have to form stiff peaks.

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:20.620
See, this is correct now.

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:24.610
See, they hold in the beater and they make those little peaks.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:28.320
So now they're ready to be folded into the souffle mixture.

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:42.040
and you want to you want to take a quarter of the egg whites and frankly beat them into your

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:48.950
chocolate base and this is to make the chocolate base a little bit lighter you do this almost

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:54.340
always when you're using egg whites because if you don't lighten your base particularly when it's

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:59.860
chocolate. It'll be so stiff that you'll deflate the egg.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.220
Then put all the rest of the egg whites on top.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:07.960
You see these, when they're properly beaten,

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:10.640
they have this lovely creamy look.

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:13.570
Then we have the folding.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:16.830
Now this is the two most important parts of the souffle.

00:22:17.570 --> 00:22:19.890
One, that you beat the egg whites properly,

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:22.330
so they form these lovely velvety peaks,

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:25.010
and two, that you fold them in properly.

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:28.760
You use a rubber spatula, and you come right down on top,

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:29.970
and then...

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:32.240
to the edge of the bowl and around.

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:36.140
You see, you come to the edge and then turn your spatula

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:39.920
and around and you're bringing a little bit of the mixture,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:43.950
the heavier mixture, up on top of the egg whites.

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:47.360
And this is your most important part.

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:50.060
As I said, the egg whites will do it for you,

00:22:50.100 --> 00:22:52.220
but you've got to do right by them

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:54.680
if you want to have them act for you.

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:58.180
And then keep turning your bowl around.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:05.620
and it's better to not blend too thoroughly than to over-blend.

00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:13.820
There, and now we have our mold, which is all ready,

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:16.220
and in it goes.

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:28.400
As you see with this, this is now really filled the mold,

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:29.960
so you just have to have that little...

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:38.690
taller there. Then you have to really plan out if you're going to have a souffle for

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:43.690
dinner. A mold this size, this is six inches high and about six and a half to seven and

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:54.390
seven across. It's going to take 55 minutes in a 375 degree oven. So you just count on

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:58.310
that and you have to plan your dinner so that your dessert will be ready at that time because

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:01.890
You just have to wait for the souffle.

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:04.250
The souffle won't wait for you,

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:05.530
because when it's ready to be eaten,

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:07.910
you've got to eat it within about two or three minutes.

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:12.670
You can get your souffle mold filled like this,

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:16.050
and it can sit out for half an hour with perfect safety.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:20.510
You just have to cover it so that it's free from drafts.

00:24:20.510 --> 00:24:23.510
So just take a big kettle and put the cover over it,

00:24:23.590 --> 00:24:26.610
and it can sit that way for half an hour,

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:29.830
and nothing will happen if your egg whites have been properly beaten.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.030
And remember that if you don't have a copper bowl,

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:38.910
remember to use that little tiny bit of cream of tartar

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:40.910
in your egg whites,

00:24:40.930 --> 00:24:43.330
because that will keep the egg whites up.

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:47.460
And so, if you have waited for half an hour,

00:24:47.500 --> 00:24:49.620
or if you've just filled the souffle

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:52.040
and are really ready to start it,

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:55.520
you would always, and this is particularly a good idea,

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:59.160
having this kind of a mold that is flame-proof,

00:24:59.160 --> 00:24:59.980
set it

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:06.100
overheat for about a minute just to let the mold start heating up and that gives your souffle a

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:10.820
little bit of push that steam is some of the chocolate or the butter which has come which

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:18.380
has come down so this would be the way you'd start it but i'm not because i have this other

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:26.080
souffle in the oven i'm not going to start it right now i'm going to let it rest free from

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:29.600
breaths. Now again I'll give you those ingredients that's

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:36.760
one third cup of sifted cornstarch, one and a half cups of milk, one half cup of sugar,

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:45.400
and then three tablespoons of butter. And then you have six ounces of semi-sweet baking chocolate

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:53.550
melted in three tablespoons of strong coffee. You have five egg yolks beaten into your chocolate

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:58.730
and sauce-based mixture, and then you have seven egg whites, and that's it.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:06.240
So we are now ready to take out this souffle that has been in our maximum security oven.

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:18.340
You shouldn't open the oven until the souffle is well risen, because at least for 35 minutes.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:24.720
Because if you do, the souffle might collapse because the oven heat goes down.

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:29.460
And you can tell when the souffle is done by taking a very sharp knife, opening the

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:29.940
oven quick.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.320
quickly after about 40 minutes and going down the side of the puff and if it comes out clean

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:44.160
your souffle is done and this is very important because particularly with a collar like this

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:51.960
if your souffle isn't done enough it's not going to support the collar. Now here's our

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:59.980
pin you remember we have the head down and the color just comes off

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.640
because it was well buttered.

00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:04.730
Now supposing you have to serve the souffle,

00:27:04.870 --> 00:27:07.070
and you can see when you're taking the collar off

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.010
that it's going to begin to collapse.

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:10.430
Well, don't you collapse.

00:27:10.570 --> 00:27:12.750
Just have a napkin ready, and if that happens,

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:15.850
just take the napkin and fold it around, collar and all,

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:18.210
and take it right into the dining room.

00:27:19.060 --> 00:27:22.460
And so now this is all ready, and so you do not see,

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:24.820
you can see it's beginning to sink very slightly,

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.460
so you just want to hurry into the dining room,

00:27:28.160 --> 00:27:29.960
gliding softly so as not to disturb.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:42.460
serve it. Here I am gliding softly into place and we will now serve the souffle. And there's

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:48.920
a trick to serving it, remember this, take your spoon and fork back to back and go click

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:58.440
into the center, separating it. If you went down too fast, I mean if you didn't separate

00:27:58.440 --> 00:27:59.600
it like this it would collapse.

00:28:00.320 --> 00:28:06.950
and not look well as you were serving it there you are that looks simply lovely and with this

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:12.740
i think it's very nice to serve a little bit of lightly whipped cream you'll find that you

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:20.170
probably need it so i'll serve another one see this will very nicely serve six but very often

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:27.780
you'll find that it really is only enough for four because everyone likes to eat it so much so

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:29.980
So, there we are with the souffle.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:31.580
and the whipped cream.

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:38.230
And with this menu, soufflé at the end,

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:41.070
you could serve a coquille Saint-Jacques.

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.610
Those would be scallops baked in their shells

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:45.370
in a lovely white wine sauce.

00:28:45.570 --> 00:28:48.650
And then you could follow that with broiled chicken,

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:50.250
plain or saucy.

00:28:50.430 --> 00:28:55.950
And you could have fresh green peas done the French way

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:58.890
and serve a rosé or a red wine.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.040
Let's take a look at this terrifying monster,

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:04.580
this mystery souffle.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:06.870
And what is it after all?

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:08.830
Well, it's melted chocolate.

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:10.140
We've done that before.

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:12.640
It's white sauce, we've done that.

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:15.640
As a matter of fact, I have news for you.

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:18.580
You've known how to make a chocolate souffle all along.

00:29:19.060 --> 00:29:22.040
So that's all for today on The French Chef.

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:23.720
This is Julia Child.

00:29:24.340 --> 00:29:25.200
Bon appétit.

